# Extended Configuration Parser with Section Support

To support `[section]` headers, we need to modify the parsing state machine. When encountering a line starting with `[` and ending with `]`, we update a mutable variable tracking the current active section. Subsequent key-value pairs are then prefixed with this section name.

If no section is currently active, keys remain unprefixed (global scope). If a new section header appears, subsequent keys belong to that new section until another header is encountered or the file ends.

Below is the complete, updated code including the modified parser logic and comprehensive unit tests.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::fmt::{self, Display};
use std::io::{self, Read};

/// Represents all possible errors that can occur during configuration parsing.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum ConfigError {
    /// Raised when a line cannot be parsed as `key = value` or `[section]`.
    Syntax(String),
    /// Raised when a duplicate key is encountered.
    DuplicateKey(String),
    /// Raised when there is an underlying I/O error while reading the source.
    Io(io::Error),
}

impl Display for ConfigError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        match self {
            ConfigError::Syntax(line) => write!(f, "Invalid syntax in line: '{}'", line),
            ConfigError::DuplicateKey(key) => write!(f, "Duplicate key found: '{}'", key),
            ConfigError::Io(err) => write!(f, "I/O error: {}", err),
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for ConfigError {}

impl From<io::Error> for ConfigError {
    fn from(err: io::Error) -> Self {
        ConfigError::Io(err)
    }
}

/// Parses a configuration string into a map of key-value pairs, supporting sections.
///
/// Lines starting with `#` and empty/whitespace-only lines are ignored.
/// Section headers are defined as `[name]`. Keys following a section header
/// are stored with the prefix `name.`. Keys before any section header are global.
/// Valid key-value lines must follow the format `key = value`.
/// Leading/trailing whitespace around keys, values, and section names is trimmed.
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `content` - The full text content of the configuration file.
///
/// # Returns
/// * `Ok(HashMap)` containing the parsed entries.
/// * `Err(ConfigError)` if any line is syntactically invalid or contains duplicate keys.
pub fn parse_config(content: &str) -> Result<HashMap<String, String>, ConfigError> {
    let mut map = HashMap::new();
    let mut current_section: Option<String> = None;

    for line in content.lines() {
        let trimmed_line = line.trim();

        // Skip empty lines and comments
        if trimmed_line.is_empty() || trimmed_line.starts_with('#') {
            continue;
        }

        // Check for section header: [name]
        if trimmed_line.starts_with('[') && trimmed_line.ends_with(']') {
            let section_name_raw = &trimmed_line[1..trimmed_line.len() - 1];
            let section_name = section_name_raw.trim().to_string();

            if section_name.is_empty() {
                return Err(ConfigError::Syntax(format!("Empty section name in line: '{}'", trimmed_line)));
            }

            current_section = Some(section_name);
            continue;
        }

        // Attempt to find the separator '=' for key-value pairs
        let eq_index = trimmed_line.find('=').ok_or_else(|| {
            ConfigError::Syntax(trimmed_line.to_string())
        })?;

        let raw_key = &trimmed_line[..eq_index];
        let raw_value = &trimmed_line[eq_index + 1..];

        let base_key = raw_key.trim().to_string();
        let value = raw_value.trim().to_string();

        // Validation: Base key should not be empty after trimming
        if base_key.is_empty() {
            return Err(ConfigError::Syntax(format!("Empty key in line: '{}'", trimmed_line)));
        }

        // Construct final key based on current section context
        let final_key = match &current_section {
            Some(section) => format!("{}.{}", section, base_key),
            None => base_key,
        };

        // Check for duplicates before inserting
        if map.contains_key(&final_key) {
            return Err(ConfigError::DuplicateKey(final_key));
        }

        map.insert(final_key, value);
    }

    Ok(map)
}

/// Reads configuration from a readable stream (e.g., a File) and parses it.
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `reader` - Any type implementing `std::io::Read`.
///
/// # Returns
/// * `Ok(HashMap)` containing the parsed entries.
/// * `Err(ConfigError)` if reading fails or parsing encounters invalid syntax/duplicates.
pub fn parse_config_from_reader<R: Read>(mut reader: R) -> Result<HashMap<String, String>, ConfigError> {
    let mut buffer = String::new();
    reader.read_to_string(&mut buffer)?;

    parse_config(&buffer)
}

